gjxdiff 0.8.1 — performance release (2026-08-05)

The static musl binary now carries its own x86-64 memcpy/memmove
implementation instead of musl's. musl's memcpy pays a fixed startup
cost per call that dominated the millions of small field- and
record-sized copies a diff performs; replacing it brings the shipped
binary on par with a glibc build.

How much faster it runs depends on how much of the run is CPU work.
Measured against 0.8.0 on the same inputs and the same machine:
1.7-1.9x on CPU-bound comparisons from 83 MB to 821 MB per side
(1.94x on an 821 MB single-document JSON pair, 1.89x warm), 1.35x on
a 2 MB pair, and about 1.3x on a 1.36 GB per side NDJSON pair, where
the run reads 2.7 GB, spools 1.55 GB of temporary files and the wall
clock is dominated by I/O. On that largest pair under a 512 MiB
memory limit the two builds are indistinguishable.

The comparison engine is unchanged. Reports, exit codes, disclosures
and memory behavior are the same as 0.8.0, with one deliberate
exception: the machine report's meta line carries the tool version,
so its "tool" field now reads "0.8.1". Everything after that first
line is byte-identical to 0.8.0 on every input tested. Golden-file
tests that pin a whole report need their first line refreshed.

