gjxdiff 0.8.0 — first public release (2026-08-04)

gjxdiff is a command-line tool for structural comparison of JSON and
NDJSON files of any size. It memory-maps its inputs, keeps working
state in temporary files under a fixed memory budget (default 4 GiB,
adjustable with --memory-limit), and streams diff records to disk as
they are found, so multi-gigabyte files can be compared without
loading either input into RAM. The same inputs and flags produce a
byte-identical report on any machine. Output formats: an NDJSON
machine report with a versioned meta line, a color-coded human view
on terminals, a summary footer (--stat), and RFC 6902 JSON Patch
export (--patch). Records can be paired by key (--key, with
autodetection), and the comparison can be narrowed with --path and
--ignore. Exit codes: 0 identical, 1 differences found, 2 error,
130 interrupted.

This is pre-1.0 software: the report format and flags may still
change between minor releases. The report's meta line carries
"stability":"draft" until the v1 schema freeze.

Known limitations are documented in doc/MANUAL.md: section 12
(Troubleshooting) explains every disclosure the tool prints about
work it skipped, absorbed or coarsened, and section 13 (Limits at a
glance) lists the hard limits — among them: containers above the
alignment cap are reported as one coarse region each (disclosed, cap
raisable via --memory-limit or --align-cap); only whole containers
of at least 64 bytes report as moves; at most 65535 distinct report
paths; single-threaded by design; Linux x86-64 only.

Package contents: gjxdiff (static x86_64 Linux binary, musl libc),
LICENSE, README.md, doc/MANUAL.md, doc/QUICKREF.md, doc/gjxdiff.1
(man page), RELEASENOTES.

